      "status": "COMPLETED",
      "content": "That confirms it — the Al2O3 CIF is written in P1 (20 explicit atoms), and spglib correctly recovers the corundum symmetry where all 20 atoms sit on the 12i Wyckoff position. The solution is complete and verified.\n\n**Summary**\n\nThe script is at `workspace/workspace/solution.py` with the entry function `analyze_wyckoff_position_multiplicities_and_coordinates(filepath)`:\n\n1. **Reads the CIF** with `pymatgen.core.Structure.from_file`.\n2. **Gets per-atom Wyckoff letters** via `SpacegroupAnalyzer(structure).get_symmetry_dataset().wyckoffs` — using the spglib symmetry dataset (not the symmetrized structure), so each letter maps to one atom in the original cell.\n3. **Multiplicity** = count of atoms per Wyckoff letter (`Counter`).\n4. **Coordinates** of the first atom on each position (file order), rounded via `sympy.Rational(x).limit_denominator(12)` to the closest rational with denominator ≤ 12, formatted like `\"3/8\"`.\n\nThe FeS2_mp-226 example reproduces the expected output exactly:\n```json\n{\n    \"wyckoff_multiplicity_dict\": {\"a\": 4, \"c\": 8},\n    \"wyckoff_coordinates_dict\": {\n        \"a\": [\"0\", \"1/2\", \"1/2\"],\n        \"c\": [\"3/8\", \"1/9\", \"8/9\"]\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAll 11 CIF files in `workspace/cif_files/` process successfully (running the script with no arguments batch-processes the whole directory and prints JSON per file). Nothing is hardcoded — results are derived from the symmetry analysis of each file.",
      "text": "That confirms it — the Al2O3 CIF is written in P1 (20 explicit atoms), and spglib correctly recovers the corundum symmetry where all 20 atoms sit on the 12i Wyckoff position. The solution is complete and verified.\n\n**Summary**\n\nThe script is at `workspace/workspace/solution.py` with the entry function `analyze_wyckoff_position_multiplicities_and_coordinates(filepath)`:\n\n1. **Reads the CIF** with `pymatgen.core.Structure.from_file`.\n2. **Gets per-atom Wyckoff letters** via `SpacegroupAnalyzer(structure).get_symmetry_dataset().wyckoffs` — using the spglib symmetry dataset (not the symmetrized structure), so each letter maps to one atom in the original cell.\n3. **Multiplicity** = count of atoms per Wyckoff letter (`Counter`).\n4. **Coordinates** of the first atom on each position (file order), rounded via `sympy.Rational(x).limit_denominator(12)` to the closest rational with denominator ≤ 12, formatted like `\"3/8\"`.\n\nThe FeS2_mp-226 example reproduces the expected output exactly:\n```json\n{\n    \"wyckoff_multiplicity_dict\": {\"a\": 4, \"c\": 8},\n    \"wyckoff_coordinates_dict\": {\n        \"a\": [\"0\", \"1/2\", \"1/2\"],\n        \"c\": [\"3/8\", \"1/9\", \"8/9\"]\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAll 11 CIF files in `workspace/cif_files/` process successfully (running the script with no arguments batch-processes the whole directory and prints JSON per file). Nothing is hardcoded — results are derived from the symmetry analysis of each file.",
